00:01:55.310 --> 00:01:58.550
are in order to identify
them for picking.

00:01:58.550 --> 00:02:01.640
Using image recognition, we
could speed up this process

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.460
and improve its accuracy
by just bypassing

00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.940
the problem of needing to
find where the barcode is.

00:02:07.940 --> 00:02:09.070
And there is more.

00:02:09.070 --> 00:02:12.410
With such technology, we could
also provide to the customer

00:02:12.410 --> 00:02:16.310
the possibility to just take
photos of the products they

00:02:16.310 --> 00:02:18.560
want to add to their baskets.

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.590
Another example of machine
learning that can be used

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.590
is for demand forecasting.

00:02:24.590 --> 00:02:28.250
To deliver thousands of
orders per day properly,

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:30.170
we need to anticipate
the demand.

00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.890
How many orders will
be placed every day?

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:37.100
That can change during
the week, the weekend,

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:40.640
during holidays, or most
especially, on Christmas.

00:02:40.640 --> 00:02:42.980
And if you can also
more precisely predict

00:02:42.980 --> 00:02:48.770
how many products of each kind
will be demanded every day,

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.920
you will also better
anticipate the supply chain.

00:02:51.920 --> 00:02:55.830
And in these kind of problems,
machine learning can help.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.680
For example, product
recommendation.

00:02:58.680 --> 00:03:01.310
Have you run out of some
of your regular products?

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:03.720
Would you also like some
complimentary products

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:05.550
with the one in your baskets?

00:03:05.550 --> 00:03:09.420
Would you rather like one
product instead of another one?

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:11.340
Machine learning can help.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:14.010
And a last example that
we will detail more

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:17.130
into this presentation
is a classical problem

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:21.160
that we face in online retail,
that is fraud detection.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.680
Orders are being delivered
but never paid for,

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:30.240
or being delivered, paid
for, but then the money

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:32.370
is required back.

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:33.550
This is called chargeback.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:37.050
And this happens, in general,
when the credit card used

00:03:37.050 --> 00:03:39.790
was a stolen one.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.730
OK, now let's enter into
more detail into this fraud

00:03:43.730 --> 00:03:48.320
detection problem and
start setting the context.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:53.930
Ocado retail in UK is
more than 50,000 products,

00:03:53.930 --> 00:03:59.230
more than 650,000 customers,
more than 250,000 orders

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:00.500
per week.

00:04:00.500 --> 00:04:03.620
So imagine you only have
one failed payment out

00:04:03.620 --> 00:04:06.620
of 1,000 orders,
it already means

00:04:06.620 --> 00:04:09.270
losing millions of dollars.

00:04:09.270 --> 00:04:11.390
On the other hand,
if you can improve

00:04:11.390 --> 00:04:16.160
just by a few percentage
your fraud detection system,

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:19.529
you can save tens of
thousands of dollars.

00:04:19.529 --> 00:04:22.470
With one big warning,
false positive.

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:25.810
If you predict that one
order will be fraudulent

00:04:25.810 --> 00:04:28.170
and it's not because
it's a regular customer,

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:34.160
this has higher costs, because
a regular customer that

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.630
will stop buying with you is a
lot of possible future orders

00:04:38.630 --> 00:04:40.760
that you are losing,
and not just one,

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:41.800
and possibly some fail.

00:04:44.430 --> 00:04:46.980
And so I told you,
now we have on board

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:49.769
much more retailers from
all around the world.

00:04:49.769 --> 00:04:51.060
So these numbers just multiply.

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:57.310
OK, so how can we face this kind
of problem of fraud detection?

00:04:57.310 --> 00:05:03.340
Historically, we have been
using a set of handmade rules,

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.230
set of rules, like
for example, if you

00:05:05.230 --> 00:05:10.450
place more than three orders
for the future for more than 500

00:05:10.450 --> 00:05:14.750
pounds and you let us less
than 24 hours for the delivery,

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:17.410
well, your order is risky.

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:20.680
You combine a lot
of rules like that,

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:26.500
you give a score that gives a
risk potential of your orders.

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:29.620
So this kind of system
is based on intuitions,

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:31.150
human intuitions.

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:34.930
Humans can, in general,
define 10, 20, 30

00:05:34.930 --> 00:05:37.600
rules based on 10, 20 features.

00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:40.840
Each rule is based on
1, 2, 3, 4 conditions.

00:05:40.840 --> 00:05:44.760
And in general, it's kind
of fixed set of rules.

00:05:44.760 --> 00:05:48.280
Other possible approach
is machine learning.

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:52.270
If you derive features
from your incoming orders,

00:05:52.270 --> 00:05:55.750
like how many first orders
of the customer, what's

00:05:55.750 --> 00:05:59.380
the total amount of
your baskets, what's

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:01.900
the time between the order
and the delivery, and you

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:05.440
pass all these features
along with the known class

00:06:05.440 --> 00:06:08.860
of failed payments or not to a
machine learning algorithm that

00:06:08.860 --> 00:06:10.570
will learn a model,
that will then

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:13.780
be able to predict if a
new incoming order will

00:06:13.780 --> 00:06:16.922
be associated with
failed payments or not.

00:06:16.922 --> 00:06:20.620
This system is based on
data instead of intuitions

00:06:20.620 --> 00:06:23.950
and can handle much more
rules, much more features,

00:06:23.950 --> 00:06:26.240
much more condition on rules.

00:06:26.240 --> 00:06:27.700
And it is adaptable.

00:06:27.700 --> 00:06:32.080
You can relearn regularly
your machine learning model,

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:36.850
and it will take into account
the last behavior of fraudster

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:41.730
that can evolve with time.

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:46.340
OK, so we ran some analysis
on the different machine

00:06:46.340 --> 00:06:48.330
learning algorithm available.

00:06:48.330 --> 00:06:54.180
And I put here on two axes
the results that we have.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:58.020
On the vertical axis is
the predictive performance

00:06:58.020 --> 00:07:00.940
of the models.

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:03.420
And extra boosts,
like many times

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.420
in Kaggle competition
and real life exhibits,

00:07:06.420 --> 00:07:09.090
is reaching the best
predictive performance.

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:10.650
And then TensorFlow
neural network

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:13.170
has also interesting results.

00:07:13.170 --> 00:07:16.740
And I put on the horizontal
axis another aspect

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:18.900
that is important
in fraud detection.

00:07:18.900 --> 00:07:21.030
That is explainability.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.000
If your algorithm
can explain why

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:27.990
he thinks that one order is
fraudulent or not, for example,

00:07:27.990 --> 00:07:33.950
because the content of
the basket is not normal,

00:07:33.950 --> 00:07:41.070
or if the payment methods
used seems suspicious,

00:07:41.070 --> 00:07:42.850
it will help the human.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:45.900
And if you want to use this
machine learning algorithm

00:07:45.900 --> 00:07:48.900
to make automated decisions
for legal reasons,

00:07:48.900 --> 00:07:52.720
you need to explain why
it rejected one order.

00:07:52.720 --> 00:07:54.510
So obviously, on
these certain aspects,

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:57.580
the handmade rules are the best.

00:07:57.580 --> 00:08:00.000
It's more understandable
for humans.

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.130
But the rule induction
algorithms are really close.

00:08:03.130 --> 00:08:05.490
Decision tree also
are understandable,

00:08:05.490 --> 00:08:10.710
but sometimes the depth of the
tree is too high for a human

00:08:10.710 --> 00:08:14.210
to really apprehend it.

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:16.030
OK, here is a really
important thing.

00:08:16.030 --> 00:08:17.590
I am a data scientist.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:20.990
I know quite a lot about machine
learning, neural networks,

00:08:20.990 --> 00:08:23.690
how to cluster data,
deduplicate data, how

00:08:23.690 --> 00:08:25.870
to face an unbalanced
class problem,

00:08:25.870 --> 00:08:29.150
how to configure parameters of
neural networks, all of those,

00:08:29.150 --> 00:08:32.000
but I don't know quite anything
about machine settings,

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:35.020
networks topology, clustering
machine, replicate data, load

00:08:35.020 --> 00:08:37.940
balancing, configure server.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:40.100
So the main question
here is, how can I

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:44.600
now go from my big data lying
some data lake into a fully

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:48.310
automated pipeline of machine
learning serving predictions

00:08:48.310 --> 00:08:51.980
for fraud detection in
production, hopefully

00:08:51.980 --> 00:08:54.560
easily and quickly,
but most importantly,

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:57.410
scaling and with
no need to set up

00:08:57.410 --> 00:09:00.870
a lot of machines and containers
and auto-scaling processes, et

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:03.170
cetera.

00:09:03.170 --> 00:09:07.050
With one main constraint,
that is real time.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:08.760
Fraudsters don't lose time.

00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:10.680
If they steal a
credit card, they

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:16.770
will try to use it to place
five orders for a high amount

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:18.930
and for short notice.

00:09:18.930 --> 00:09:22.350
So imagine we have a model that
just used two features-- how

00:09:22.350 --> 00:09:25.080
many past orders for the
customer and how many

00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:27.330
future orders for the customer.

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:29.470
At training time, it's easy.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:31.920
We have the access to all data.

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:34.683
We can deride the
future from the inputs,

00:09:34.683 --> 00:09:38.160
pass it to the machine
learning model algorithm that

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:39.540
will learn a model.

00:09:39.540 --> 00:09:42.450
At serving time now, it
can be more complicated.

00:09:42.450 --> 00:09:44.460
The number of past
orders can be something

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:48.150
that has been pre-computed and
exposed to get the information

00:09:48.150 --> 00:09:50.550
directly at real time.

00:09:50.550 --> 00:09:53.730
The number of future orders
can be more complicated,

00:09:53.730 --> 00:09:57.900
because since the fraudster
can place a lot in real time,

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:59.530
you need to be
closer to real time.

00:09:59.530 --> 00:10:05.220
So you need one set of features
pre-computed, another one

00:10:05.220 --> 00:10:09.420
in real time, and combine
them to provide as inputs

00:10:09.420 --> 00:10:12.000
to the machine learning
model exactly the same

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:14.340
as training time.

00:10:14.340 --> 00:10:17.550
So [INAUDIBLE],, do you
have some good solution

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:22.922
to handle my problems and
maybe with some Google tools?

00:10:22.922 --> 00:10:24.630
PRZEMYSLAW PASTUSZKA:
Thank you, Laurent.

00:10:24.630 --> 00:10:27.090
I will try to do just that.

00:10:27.090 --> 00:10:29.750
First of all, thank
you for coming.

00:10:29.750 --> 00:10:33.360
And we will now see how we
can implement the solution

00:10:33.360 --> 00:10:36.570
for a fraud detection problem
using Google Cloud Platform.

00:10:36.570 --> 00:10:38.250
And of course, as
Laurent said, we

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.800
would like it to be
as easy as possible.

00:10:41.800 --> 00:10:44.400
So let's do this.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:46.570
Well, first of all,
I would like you

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.480
to imagine where we want to
be at the end of the journey.

00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:52.730
What are the actual
physical components

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:55.640
that will be in play here?

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:58.570
So on the left side
here, what you can see

00:10:58.570 --> 00:11:01.180
is order management
microservice.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.970
Ocada's smart platform
is powered by hundreds

00:11:03.970 --> 00:11:06.370
of microservices like this.

00:11:06.370 --> 00:11:10.370
Each of them has very clearly
defined responsibility.

00:11:10.370 --> 00:11:13.540
And here, as the name
suggests, order management

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:16.820
deals with orders being placed.

00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:20.210
This service, when the
order is being placed,

00:11:20.210 --> 00:11:23.990
would like to know whether this
order is fraudulent or not.

00:11:23.990 --> 00:11:27.560
So what it will do, it will
consult other microservice--

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:29.960
let's call it fraud service--

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:34.310
by sending it customer ID and
all details about the order.

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:36.350
And what it would
like to get in return

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:41.240
is what is the probability of
this order being fraudulent?

00:11:41.240 --> 00:11:43.760
And the fraud service is
actually the component

00:11:43.760 --> 00:11:45.500
that we are trying to build.

00:11:45.500 --> 00:11:48.200
So please keep
this image in mind.

00:11:48.200 --> 00:11:50.120
So this is where
we are heading to.

00:11:53.750 --> 00:11:56.690
What if you've never done
machine learning project?

00:11:56.690 --> 00:11:58.970
How would you even start?

00:11:58.970 --> 00:12:00.890
Where do you begin?

00:12:00.890 --> 00:12:04.450
So of course, there is no
machine learning without data.

00:12:04.450 --> 00:12:06.170
You need a fair
amount of history

00:12:06.170 --> 00:12:10.640
to be able to learn
patterns from the behaviors

00:12:10.640 --> 00:12:12.960
of your customers.

00:12:12.960 --> 00:12:15.630
To do that, you need
the right set of tools

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:18.870
to store and process the data.

00:12:18.870 --> 00:12:23.190
And since we're talking
about Google Cloud Platform,

00:12:23.190 --> 00:12:26.400
there is a really good
analytical database

00:12:26.400 --> 00:12:27.760
that we would like to use.

00:12:27.760 --> 00:12:30.560
And it's Google BigQuery.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:34.260
We, as Ocado, store all
our data in BigQuery.

00:12:34.260 --> 00:12:38.220
So it's petabytes
of data, actually.

00:12:38.220 --> 00:12:42.690
And BigQuery scales to
this amount seamlessly.

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:44.820
The other thing that we
really like about BigQuery

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:47.130
is that how fast it is.

00:12:47.130 --> 00:12:51.780
We can write SQL queries, run
them over terabytes of data,

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:55.990
and still get results in terms
of minutes or even seconds.

00:12:55.990 --> 00:12:57.940
The other good thing
is that it doesn't

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:04.300
require you to have separate
DBA team to manage BigQuery.

00:13:04.300 --> 00:13:07.210
You just load data into
BigQuery, you run queries,

00:13:07.210 --> 00:13:10.410
and that's all that is there.

00:13:10.410 --> 00:13:14.030
And the last thing that
kind of sold BigQuery to us

00:13:14.030 --> 00:13:16.130
is the pricing policy.

00:13:16.130 --> 00:13:18.230
In BigQuery, you
pay for storage.

00:13:18.230 --> 00:13:20.390
And in terms of SQL
queries, you pay

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:23.870
for the amount of data
those queries have read.

00:13:23.870 --> 00:13:27.320
So it's a very fair
policy and really

00:13:27.320 --> 00:13:32.000
helps us to control
the cost that we

00:13:32.000 --> 00:13:33.710
spend on doing analytics.

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:38.380
And there are two
questions remaining.

00:13:38.380 --> 00:13:41.937
So we decided that we ought to
use BigQuery as a main storage.

00:13:41.937 --> 00:13:43.770
So the first question
is, how do we actually

00:13:43.770 --> 00:13:45.720
load the data in BigQuery?

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:48.330
And the one which is
even more fundamental,

00:13:48.330 --> 00:13:50.960
what data you land in BigQuery?

00:13:50.960 --> 00:13:53.940
So in the data lake, you would
like to store all the data,

00:13:53.940 --> 00:13:58.340
but what all means in this case?

00:13:58.340 --> 00:14:01.170
So the rule of thumb that we
use in Ocado which is really

00:14:01.170 --> 00:14:04.110
working very well for
us is that when there

00:14:04.110 --> 00:14:08.070
is any change to the business
state of the application,

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:10.860
you should send, or
rather your microservice

00:14:10.860 --> 00:14:13.410
responsible for
that business state

00:14:13.410 --> 00:14:17.610
should send an event to
a distributed queue which

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:20.070
corresponds to the change.

00:14:20.070 --> 00:14:23.310
When we gather all the
data of the small deltas,

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:26.100
then when we go
back in time, you

00:14:26.100 --> 00:14:29.250
are able to see what
was the exact state

00:14:29.250 --> 00:14:34.570
of the whole system at any
particular point in time.

00:14:34.570 --> 00:14:38.280
So here is the diagram of
the ingestion pipeline.

00:14:38.280 --> 00:14:40.184
On the left, you see
all those microservices

00:14:40.184 --> 00:14:41.100
that I told you about.

00:14:41.100 --> 00:14:44.850
They are actually running
in Amazon Web Services.

00:14:44.850 --> 00:14:51.330
And we use AWS Kinesis
for distributive queue.

00:14:51.330 --> 00:14:52.920
Then there is a
question, how do we

00:14:52.920 --> 00:14:56.550
transport data from a
distributed queue to BigQuery?

00:14:56.550 --> 00:14:59.670
And we wrote a
streaming application

00:14:59.670 --> 00:15:03.150
using Apache Beam
and Google Dataflow,

00:15:03.150 --> 00:15:05.220
which is running 24/7.

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:08.310
And it's moving the
data in real time.

00:15:08.310 --> 00:15:10.740
And then we used streaming
inserts into BigQuery

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.140
to store this data.

00:15:13.140 --> 00:15:16.470
If you don't know what Apache
Beam or Google Dataflow is,

00:15:16.470 --> 00:15:23.400
let me spend a few minutes on
telling you more about this.

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:27.630
For that, I will
use a toy example.

00:15:27.630 --> 00:15:31.560
Let's imagine you have
a collection of words.

00:15:31.560 --> 00:15:34.290
Maybe you took a
Shakespeare novel

00:15:34.290 --> 00:15:39.760
and you split it to have an each
word that isn't in this novel.

00:15:39.760 --> 00:15:41.640
And you would like to
count how many of those

00:15:41.640 --> 00:15:47.830
words start with the letter
A, or letter B, C, et cetera.

00:15:47.830 --> 00:15:52.720
If you were to write this using,
for example, Java Streams,

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:54.220
you would probably
write a snippet

00:15:54.220 --> 00:15:56.570
like the one on the top.

00:15:56.570 --> 00:15:59.080
You would first extract
first character out

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:01.690
of each word, group
by this character,

00:16:01.690 --> 00:16:04.360
and then for each
group count the amount

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:06.520
of items in this group.

00:16:06.520 --> 00:16:09.710
And in Apache Beam,
it's very similar.

00:16:09.710 --> 00:16:14.290
You just write exactly
the same transformations.

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:17.340
You have a map which
extracts first character out

00:16:17.340 --> 00:16:21.320
of the word, you group
by, and then you count.

00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:25.220
And Apache Beam is just
a programming model

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:27.480
for transforming the data.

00:16:27.480 --> 00:16:31.520
It kind of like a Java Streams,
because how it actually

00:16:31.520 --> 00:16:35.257
executes is dependent on
JVM in case of Java Streams.

00:16:35.257 --> 00:16:36.590
And it's the same way with beam.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:39.470
You just write this
code, and how you execute

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:43.640
it depends on the execution
framework that you choose.

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:46.130
And there are plenty of
executions for a framework

00:16:46.130 --> 00:16:49.340
that you can choose from,
ranging from Apache Apex

00:16:49.340 --> 00:16:51.230
to Apache Gearpump.

00:16:51.230 --> 00:16:53.480
The one that use
is Google Dataflow,

00:16:53.480 --> 00:16:57.380
mostly because it's well
integrated with other Google

00:16:57.380 --> 00:16:58.790
Cloud Services.

00:16:58.790 --> 00:17:02.240
But it's also semi-managed.

00:17:02.240 --> 00:17:06.619
You still have control over
machine's configuration,

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:08.950
but most of the time, you
just write the Java code,

00:17:08.950 --> 00:17:13.230
you upload to Google
Dataflow, and it just runs.

00:17:13.230 --> 00:17:16.400
Right, we have all
our data in BigQuery.

00:17:16.400 --> 00:17:19.990
So the next app that Laurent
or anyone working on machine

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:22.210
learning algorithm
would do is probably

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:25.480
to go there and explore
what data's available.

00:17:25.480 --> 00:17:28.089
You need to see what
are the distributions

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:32.410
of different variables,
how different tables join

00:17:32.410 --> 00:17:33.590
with each other.

00:17:33.590 --> 00:17:37.160
You can ask different
questions about your data.

00:17:37.160 --> 00:17:40.810
For example, you can
ask whether people

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:44.170
that have a lot amount of
alcohol in their orders

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:48.100
are more likely to be fraudsters
than the other people.

00:17:48.100 --> 00:17:51.700
And the really great tool to
do that exploratory analysis

00:17:51.700 --> 00:17:55.030
is BigQuery, because
first of all, we already

00:17:55.030 --> 00:17:57.080
have data there.

00:17:57.080 --> 00:18:01.720
It has web UI, where it can
just go, type the query,

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:03.670
and get a result fast.

00:18:03.670 --> 00:18:07.030
And based on the results, you
can formulate another query

00:18:07.030 --> 00:18:10.600
and iterate, iterate, to
the point where you have all

00:18:10.600 --> 00:18:13.850
the answers for your questions.

00:18:13.850 --> 00:18:18.530
If you need to do a
more deep analysis,

00:18:18.530 --> 00:18:21.160
maybe plot some charts,
share the results

00:18:21.160 --> 00:18:24.860
with another people,
there are other tools

00:18:24.860 --> 00:18:27.680
in Google Cloud Platform
that allow you to do that.

00:18:27.680 --> 00:18:32.780
One I would like to
mention is Google Datalab,

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:35.690
which is basically a
hosted Jupyter notebook.

00:18:35.690 --> 00:18:38.480
You write Python code there.

00:18:38.480 --> 00:18:40.190
You can extract
data from BigQuery.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:43.730
You can plot charts
with whatever libraries

00:18:43.730 --> 00:18:47.509
that are available in
Python environment.

00:18:47.509 --> 00:18:48.300
You can use Pandas.

00:18:48.300 --> 00:18:49.390
You can use scikit-learn.

00:18:49.390 --> 00:18:52.120
You can use Plotly,
whatever you want.

00:18:52.120 --> 00:18:55.130
And there is also another
tool from Google called Google

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:57.110
Collaborator that
you see here, which

00:18:57.110 --> 00:19:01.020
is kind of taking this idea
of notebooks one step further.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.230
So in Google Collaborator,
the Jupyter notebook

00:19:03.230 --> 00:19:05.720
is just a file on Google Drive.

00:19:05.720 --> 00:19:09.380
And you can do whatever you
do with Google Drive files

00:19:09.380 --> 00:19:11.090
with Jupyter notebook here.

00:19:11.090 --> 00:19:12.410
So you can share it.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:14.210
You can put comments in here.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:16.730
Many people can collaborate
on the same notebook

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:18.170
at the same time.

00:19:18.170 --> 00:19:20.810
We use it a lot in Ocado,
especially if we're

00:19:20.810 --> 00:19:22.790
doing reviews on notebooks.

00:19:22.790 --> 00:19:26.460
And we find it very handy.

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:30.276
At this point, we are
ready to jump into ML.

00:19:30.276 --> 00:19:31.650
We will do a
feature engineering,

00:19:31.650 --> 00:19:35.620
so we'll extract meaningful
information out of raw data,

00:19:35.620 --> 00:19:38.810
and we'll train and model.

00:19:38.810 --> 00:19:41.860
But first, which
model do you choose?

00:19:41.860 --> 00:19:44.680
Here's the slide that
Laurent already showed you.

00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:46.210
And Laurent told
you that there are

00:19:46.210 --> 00:19:48.130
two things that we care about.

00:19:48.130 --> 00:19:50.860
One is accuracy on the y-axis.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.930
And the other one
is explainability,

00:19:52.930 --> 00:19:56.180
which you can see
here on the x-axis.

00:19:56.180 --> 00:20:00.110
But to be honest, there is also
a third dimension here which

00:20:00.110 --> 00:20:01.620
we need to take into account.

00:20:01.620 --> 00:20:04.730
And this is how
easy it is to deploy

00:20:04.730 --> 00:20:07.790
those solutions to the cloud.

00:20:07.790 --> 00:20:12.440
Google Cloud Platform offers
you a cloud machine learning.

00:20:12.440 --> 00:20:14.660
This is a service that
was first designed

00:20:14.660 --> 00:20:20.470
to train and serve neural
nets, written in TensorFlow.

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:22.210
How this works exactly?

00:20:22.210 --> 00:20:24.100
You write your TensorFlow code.

00:20:24.100 --> 00:20:27.550
You package it into
a Python package.

00:20:27.550 --> 00:20:30.850
You put it on Google
Cloud Storage.

00:20:30.850 --> 00:20:33.570
And then you just call it
cloud machine learning API.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:36.130
You say, here's my
package with TensorFlow.

00:20:36.130 --> 00:20:37.210
Here's the input data.

00:20:37.210 --> 00:20:39.110
Please train the model for me.

00:20:39.110 --> 00:20:42.550
And then the trained
model is just another file

00:20:42.550 --> 00:20:43.990
on Google Cloud Storage.

00:20:43.990 --> 00:20:47.240
So then you can point cloud
machine learning to this model.

00:20:47.240 --> 00:20:50.105
And you can say,
please serve it for me.

00:20:50.105 --> 00:20:51.730
So what cloud machine
learning will do,

00:20:51.730 --> 00:20:55.470
it will create a REST endpoint
that you can call in real time.

00:20:55.470 --> 00:20:56.980
You just give it
all the features

00:20:56.980 --> 00:20:59.470
and you get predictions back.

00:20:59.470 --> 00:21:02.365
Sometime ago, Google announced
that cloud machine learning

00:21:02.365 --> 00:21:07.480
will support other algorithms,
except of TensorFlow.

00:21:07.480 --> 00:21:10.390
So there is support
for serving XGBoost

00:21:10.390 --> 00:21:12.580
and scikit-learn models.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:16.630
Unfortunately, there is no way
to train them yet on the cloud.

00:21:16.630 --> 00:21:18.490
And of course, you
can use CPUs, GPUs

00:21:18.490 --> 00:21:22.820
for training all this stuff.

00:21:22.820 --> 00:21:25.820
So to sum up, the
neural networks

00:21:25.820 --> 00:21:28.250
are now really easy to
deploy to the cloud thanks

00:21:28.250 --> 00:21:31.200
to cloud machine learning.

00:21:31.200 --> 00:21:35.220
It's not so easy when it comes
to XGBoost and scikit-learn,

00:21:35.220 --> 00:21:37.500
because you still need to
take care of the training

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.720
by yourself.

00:21:39.720 --> 00:21:44.870
And if you were to use the
rule induction, the C5 program,

00:21:44.870 --> 00:21:48.870
which it was written in C++, we
really have no out of the box

00:21:48.870 --> 00:21:51.074
way to run it on the cloud.

00:21:51.074 --> 00:21:52.740
If we were to do this,
we would probably

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:55.380
package it into a
Docker container,

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:58.050
run on Google Container
Engine with Kubernetes.

00:21:58.050 --> 00:21:59.820
But this will
require a lot of work

00:21:59.820 --> 00:22:03.160
from us, lot of fine tuning.

00:22:03.160 --> 00:22:05.260
So given all of
that information,

00:22:05.260 --> 00:22:09.650
we made a conscious decision
that we will sacrifice

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:11.350
explainability for now.

00:22:11.350 --> 00:22:13.210
So it's not that we
don't care about it,

00:22:13.210 --> 00:22:15.250
but to get us faster
to production,

00:22:15.250 --> 00:22:18.760
we'll start with something which
is easy to deploy to the cloud.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.550
And since neural nets are
easy to deploy and still

00:22:21.550 --> 00:22:26.590
have pretty good accuracy,
this is what we went for.

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:29.140
All, right so we have
chosen the ML model.

00:22:29.140 --> 00:22:31.100
It's now time to train it.

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:34.690
And here's how the
training pipeline

00:22:34.690 --> 00:22:37.280
looks like on the high level.

00:22:37.280 --> 00:22:39.940
So we have events that
I told you about before.

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:42.790
So each corresponds
to the single change

00:22:42.790 --> 00:22:44.600
to the business state
of the application.

00:22:44.600 --> 00:22:47.700
Out of those, we need to
extract meaningful information.

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:51.460
For example, how many failed
transactions did this customer

00:22:51.460 --> 00:22:53.480
have in the past?

00:22:53.480 --> 00:22:56.060
And for that, we use
primarily BigQuery.

00:22:56.060 --> 00:22:58.850
So we wrote SQL queries
THAT transform this data

00:22:58.850 --> 00:23:03.540
and store them in BigQuery
as a set of tables.

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:09.730
At some point, so that those
SQL became hard to manage,

00:23:09.730 --> 00:23:12.600
so that we decided to
rewrite some of those

00:23:12.600 --> 00:23:18.080
transformations into Dataflow,
again, using Apache Beam.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:21.450
And when we have all
of these in BigQuery,

00:23:21.450 --> 00:23:23.690
we can just run
Cloud ML training

00:23:23.690 --> 00:23:27.170
and obtain a trained model.

00:23:27.170 --> 00:23:28.970
Of course, in the
machine learning,

00:23:28.970 --> 00:23:30.500
it's very rare
case that you just

00:23:30.500 --> 00:23:32.360
train a model once and deploy.

00:23:32.360 --> 00:23:36.150
Usually, what you do is
you refresh the model.

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:39.210
And to do that, you need some
way to schedule and orchestrate

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:41.960
your training pipeline.

00:23:41.960 --> 00:23:45.920
When we started with
GCP five years back,

00:23:45.920 --> 00:23:48.500
there was no out
of the box solution

00:23:48.500 --> 00:23:51.740
ready in Google Cloud
Platform, so we wrote our own.

00:23:51.740 --> 00:23:54.410
We called it Query Manager, and
it's an app engine application

00:23:54.410 --> 00:23:57.980
that takes care of all of that.

00:23:57.980 --> 00:24:00.920
Fortunately for you, if
you are starting right now,

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:04.970
there is now hosted
Apache Airflow,

00:24:04.970 --> 00:24:08.450
which goes by the name
Google Cloud Composer.

00:24:08.450 --> 00:24:10.700
It's currently in
beta, but I highly

00:24:10.700 --> 00:24:14.540
encourage you to
go and try it out.

00:24:14.540 --> 00:24:18.860
So what we do, we schedule our
training pipeline to run daily.

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.780
And each day we
have a fresh model.

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:26.450
And the one piece
left is that we

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:29.340
need to serve this
model in real time.

00:24:29.340 --> 00:24:32.690
So let's see how
we do this exactly.

00:24:32.690 --> 00:24:35.180
Just to recall, this
is where we want to be.

00:24:35.180 --> 00:24:37.960
Order Management
application, as you remember,

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:41.420
sends the customer ID and
order detail to Fraud Service,

00:24:41.420 --> 00:24:44.960
and Fraud Service returns
probability of this order

00:24:44.960 --> 00:24:46.640
being fraudulent.

00:24:46.640 --> 00:24:50.560
How do we connect that with
Cloud Machine Learning?

00:24:50.560 --> 00:24:53.050
One idea is maybe
the fraud service

00:24:53.050 --> 00:24:55.180
will just send the
features to Cloud Machine

00:24:55.180 --> 00:24:58.420
Learning in real time
and we'll get a response,

00:24:58.420 --> 00:25:01.900
and it will just pass it
back to Order Management.

00:25:01.900 --> 00:25:05.380
The one problem here is that
Fraud Service doesn't know

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:07.030
all the features at this point.

00:25:07.030 --> 00:25:10.900
So it knows what's the
current order of the customer,

00:25:10.900 --> 00:25:14.030
but it doesn't know what was the
past behavior of the customer.

00:25:14.030 --> 00:25:17.420
So we need to somehow
obtain this data.

00:25:17.420 --> 00:25:19.610
If you recall, we have
already calculated

00:25:19.610 --> 00:25:23.600
all of this in BigQuery,
so we have all the features

00:25:23.600 --> 00:25:25.760
about the past behavior
of the customer ready,

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.500
sitting there in BigQuery.

00:25:27.500 --> 00:25:31.340
So maybe we'll just
make Fraud Service

00:25:31.340 --> 00:25:36.140
to call BigQuery, get the data
for this particular customer,

00:25:36.140 --> 00:25:40.790
and then combine it
with current order data

00:25:40.790 --> 00:25:43.990
and pass it along to
Cloud Machine Learning.

00:25:43.990 --> 00:25:48.070
Again, the one problem
for that is BigQuery

00:25:48.070 --> 00:25:51.100
was not really designed
for random access patterns.

00:25:51.100 --> 00:25:54.220
Going to BigQuery, finding
a row corresponding

00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:57.460
to a particular customer would
be extremely inefficient.

00:25:57.460 --> 00:25:59.710
And we cannot afford to do this.

00:25:59.710 --> 00:26:02.800
What we need is some
kind of persistent cache

00:26:02.800 --> 00:26:08.010
that will sit between BigQuery
and the Fraud Service.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.400
So what we used, we
used Google Datastore.

00:26:10.400 --> 00:26:13.480
Google Datastore is
NoSQL document database.

00:26:13.480 --> 00:26:16.940
So you put entities
there with no schema.

00:26:16.940 --> 00:26:19.820
You can easily search for them.

00:26:19.820 --> 00:26:23.240
You can have SQLite
queries over them.

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:27.260
But we don't really use
all of those features.

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:31.980
What we do is we use Google
Datastore as a key value store.

00:26:31.980 --> 00:26:37.610
So we put the data about
customers as a key value pair.

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.410
Key is the customer
ID, and the value

00:26:39.410 --> 00:26:42.900
is all the features
for this customer.

00:26:42.900 --> 00:26:46.230
So now how do we
populate the datastore?

00:26:46.230 --> 00:26:49.200
We simply extended
the training pipeline,

00:26:49.200 --> 00:26:50.870
so when the features
are calculated,

00:26:50.870 --> 00:26:54.560
we also use Google Dataflow
to transport these features

00:26:54.560 --> 00:26:57.050
from BigQuery to Datastore.

00:26:57.050 --> 00:27:00.650
And this is done in Dataflow
with Apache Beam again.

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:02.870
Since Apache Beam
already has connectors

00:27:02.870 --> 00:27:05.840
to BigQuery and
Datastore, writing

00:27:05.840 --> 00:27:09.380
a program which does this data
transformation was really easy.

00:27:09.380 --> 00:27:13.320
It's just 40 or
50 lines of code.

00:27:13.320 --> 00:27:16.590
And it runs in a batch mode
and transports the data

00:27:16.590 --> 00:27:19.790
to a datastore.

00:27:19.790 --> 00:27:23.840
If we combine all the pieces
together, training and serving,

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:25.700
this is what we end up with.

00:27:28.270 --> 00:27:31.360
If you look at the bottom
of the serving part,

00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:35.530
we, again, have Order Management
sending current features

00:27:35.530 --> 00:27:38.510
to a fraud service,
which calls Datastore

00:27:38.510 --> 00:27:41.290
to obtain the past features.

00:27:41.290 --> 00:27:44.020
Then it combines it and
pass along to Cloud ML,

00:27:44.020 --> 00:27:46.370
get the response back.

00:27:46.370 --> 00:27:50.170
So this is how it works.

00:27:50.170 --> 00:27:54.260
And we were pretty happy
with this architecture.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:57.620
The one problem here is that
the datastore is populated

00:27:57.620 --> 00:27:58.760
every day, right?

00:27:58.760 --> 00:28:02.150
It's not populated
every few seconds.

00:28:02.150 --> 00:28:04.400
Only when the training
pipeline runs.

00:28:04.400 --> 00:28:07.670
So again, the problem
that Laurent described

00:28:07.670 --> 00:28:09.860
when a fraudster
steals a credit card

00:28:09.860 --> 00:28:12.890
and she or he will try
to put many transactions

00:28:12.890 --> 00:28:16.700
in a tiny time window,
we will simply not

00:28:16.700 --> 00:28:20.400
notice this behavior
in our features.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:22.710
So we had to do a little hack.

00:28:22.710 --> 00:28:25.440
And this is the
final architecture

00:28:25.440 --> 00:28:28.990
for serving that is working
on production right now.

00:28:28.990 --> 00:28:33.530
So Fraud Service
now gets the data

00:28:33.530 --> 00:28:35.670
from Order Management,
current order.

00:28:35.670 --> 00:28:39.140
It gets past features one
day old from a datastore.

00:28:39.140 --> 00:28:42.620
But it also queries
other microservices

00:28:42.620 --> 00:28:44.440
for the latest data
about the customer.

00:28:44.440 --> 00:28:46.430
So it's all done in real time.

00:28:46.430 --> 00:28:50.330
And only then Fraud Service
combines all of this,

00:28:50.330 --> 00:28:53.570
sends request to Cloud
Machine Learning,

00:28:53.570 --> 00:28:57.060
and gets response back.

00:28:57.060 --> 00:29:00.050
All right, this is how our
architecture looks like.

00:29:00.050 --> 00:29:04.610
And now, Laurent, can you tell
us a bit more about the results

00:29:04.610 --> 00:29:05.760
that we got?

00:29:05.760 --> 00:29:07.260
LAURENT CANDILLIER: Sure.

00:29:07.260 --> 00:29:09.250
Thank you, [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:09.250 --> 00:29:10.450
That's great.

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:13.500
Basically, we have all
what we need to run

00:29:13.500 --> 00:29:16.280
machine running on the cloud.

00:29:16.280 --> 00:29:20.360
I can start with simple feature
engineering in Google BigQuery,

00:29:20.360 --> 00:29:26.960
where my data lies, extract
some simple feature,

00:29:26.960 --> 00:29:31.310
like counting the past
behavior of a customer,

00:29:31.310 --> 00:29:34.900
joining some tables.

00:29:34.900 --> 00:29:38.050
If I need to do more
complex data processing,

00:29:38.050 --> 00:29:41.530
I can use Google Apache
Beam in Google Dataflow.

00:29:41.530 --> 00:29:43.150
For example, normalization.

00:29:43.150 --> 00:29:46.000
I have to do a
loop on my columns,

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:49.280
compute the average and standard
deviation on numerical columns

00:29:49.280 --> 00:29:52.210
to compute my normalized values.

00:29:52.210 --> 00:29:54.640
On categorical features,
do some transformation,

00:29:54.640 --> 00:29:59.290
like [INAUDIBLE] coding, for
example, or just, as I say,

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:02.680
integers to the categories.

00:30:02.680 --> 00:30:04.360
And yeah, these
are some processing

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:05.890
that would have
been too complicated

00:30:05.890 --> 00:30:07.810
to do in standard SQL.

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:10.730
And with Google
Dataflow, it's easy.

00:30:10.730 --> 00:30:14.340
Next step, I can use Datastore
as the fastest access

00:30:14.340 --> 00:30:17.880
to expose my pre-computed
feature that I will then

00:30:17.880 --> 00:30:20.780
use at serving time.

00:30:20.780 --> 00:30:24.170
I use Google Cloud ML
Engine to do my training

00:30:24.170 --> 00:30:25.950
of machine learning models.

00:30:25.950 --> 00:30:28.460
And OK, let's use
neural networks.

00:30:28.460 --> 00:30:30.830
That is a good
conscious decision

00:30:30.830 --> 00:30:36.260
to go quickly on production
and get the best of all what

00:30:36.260 --> 00:30:40.090
is offered us by Google Cloud.

00:30:40.090 --> 00:30:44.140
Once I have trained my model,
I can easily expose it, again,

00:30:44.140 --> 00:30:47.050
through Google
Cloud ML Engine API,

00:30:47.050 --> 00:30:51.960
and combine it with what
I exposed in Datastore

00:30:51.960 --> 00:30:53.920
of my pre-computed feature.

00:30:53.920 --> 00:30:58.480
I can now run my prediction of
fraud detection in real time.

00:30:58.480 --> 00:31:00.970
So that's really great.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:07.270
And we are reaching really
great results with all these.

00:31:07.270 --> 00:31:09.670
Obviously, if you
can imagine, if you

00:31:09.670 --> 00:31:13.570
compare the machine running
algorithms with neural nets

00:31:13.570 --> 00:31:18.730
with the initial solution
that we had of handmade rules,

00:31:18.730 --> 00:31:23.230
the results are
really, really better.

00:31:23.230 --> 00:31:25.930
When I said predictive
performance already,

00:31:25.930 --> 00:31:28.840
it's not about accuracy,
because hopefully,

00:31:28.840 --> 00:31:31.750
failed payment is a rare event.

00:31:31.750 --> 00:31:37.300
Imagine you have one
fraud out of 100 orders.

00:31:37.300 --> 00:31:41.170
If you use accuracy to compute
your predictive performance,

00:31:41.170 --> 00:31:43.390
the model that will
just say nothing

00:31:43.390 --> 00:31:46.510
is fraud would
have 99% accuracy.

00:31:46.510 --> 00:31:49.990
In this kind of
unbalanced class problem,

00:31:49.990 --> 00:31:53.740
we use other measures, like
F score or area under the ROC

00:31:53.740 --> 00:31:57.060
curve that try to find a
trade-off between precision

00:31:57.060 --> 00:31:58.420
and recall.

00:31:58.420 --> 00:32:01.570
Recall being the
percentage of real fraud

00:32:01.570 --> 00:32:04.510
that your algorithm
managed to identify.

00:32:04.510 --> 00:32:07.720
And precision being
among the one that

00:32:07.720 --> 00:32:11.680
identified as fraud, what's the
percentage of right prediction

00:32:11.680 --> 00:32:13.690
in this one?

00:32:13.690 --> 00:32:17.170
With machine learning, we
also gain adaptability.

00:32:17.170 --> 00:32:20.080
We can relearn
regularly our models,

00:32:20.080 --> 00:32:25.600
and take it to account the
new behavior of fraudsters.

00:32:25.600 --> 00:32:30.260
And yeah, we are taking
the best of the cloud here,

00:32:30.260 --> 00:32:33.040
and we are already
ready to scale

00:32:33.040 --> 00:32:36.490
with all this architecture.

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:41.120
OK, so now we have
a great system

00:32:41.120 --> 00:32:44.390
to predict if an order
will be fraudulent or not.

00:32:44.390 --> 00:32:45.500
What do we do with that?

00:32:45.500 --> 00:32:49.310
What is the final
decision that we take?

00:32:49.310 --> 00:32:53.360
Easily, if the order is
identified as non-fraudulent,

00:32:53.360 --> 00:32:57.760
you can just let him go
through and avoid friction

00:32:57.760 --> 00:32:58.850
with the customer.

00:32:58.850 --> 00:33:02.200
This is something that
Ocado really valorates

00:33:02.200 --> 00:33:08.390
is just accept the
delivery and just accept

00:33:08.390 --> 00:33:10.760
that the payment will
be done afterwards,

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:12.920
because it's good
for the customer.

00:33:12.920 --> 00:33:16.280
And it's also easier to handle
when you have some product

00:33:16.280 --> 00:33:20.300
substitution, because what
you asked two weeks ago is not

00:33:20.300 --> 00:33:22.820
there, so we propose
you something else.

00:33:22.820 --> 00:33:25.790
And yeah, it's easier
to handle like that,

00:33:25.790 --> 00:33:28.290
and it's customer friendly.

00:33:28.290 --> 00:33:30.750
On the other hand,
if the algorithm

00:33:30.750 --> 00:33:32.970
says that an order is
probably fraudulent,

00:33:32.970 --> 00:33:34.980
what do you do with that?

00:33:34.980 --> 00:33:38.220
One possibility is
trigger pre-authorization

00:33:38.220 --> 00:33:39.510
on the credit card.

00:33:39.510 --> 00:33:45.440
So you reserve the amount
of the order in advance,

00:33:45.440 --> 00:33:48.930
and to ensure that
the customer will have

00:33:48.930 --> 00:33:51.330
the money at delivery time.

00:33:51.330 --> 00:33:54.750
You can also ask the fraud
agents to look at the order

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:56.430
and take the final decision.

00:33:56.430 --> 00:34:00.480
And this is where
explanation comes back.

00:34:00.480 --> 00:34:03.270
If you can explain
that you think

00:34:03.270 --> 00:34:08.909
this order is suspicious for
the content of the basket,

00:34:08.909 --> 00:34:10.670
or the past behavior
of the customer,

00:34:10.670 --> 00:34:14.159
or the payment methods
used, you can give this

00:34:14.159 --> 00:34:15.730
to the fraud agents.

00:34:15.730 --> 00:34:18.780
And yeah, if you want to
take this final decision

00:34:18.780 --> 00:34:20.790
of canceling an
order automatically,

00:34:20.790 --> 00:34:24.150
for legal reasons, you
have to be able to explain.

00:34:27.420 --> 00:34:30.170
Basically, I've just presented
you with the good result

00:34:30.170 --> 00:34:33.360
that we obtained
on fraud detection.

00:34:33.360 --> 00:34:36.889
I'm happy to announce that on
the other projects that we are

00:34:36.889 --> 00:34:40.010
running, product recommendation,
demand forecasting,

00:34:40.010 --> 00:34:41.989
and product
recognition, we are also

00:34:41.989 --> 00:34:45.800
reaching really good results
with an architecture that

00:34:45.800 --> 00:34:49.730
is generic enough to
be used in the same way

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:50.785
on different projects.

00:34:53.600 --> 00:34:56.350
So yeah, basically, Big
Data Machine Learning

00:34:56.350 --> 00:34:57.700
made easy on the cloud.

00:34:57.700 --> 00:34:59.320
Thank you, Google.

00:34:59.320 --> 00:35:00.940
What's next?

00:35:00.940 --> 00:35:06.790
Before taking some ideas and
questions from you, some hints.

00:35:06.790 --> 00:35:09.550
We started to play with
Google Dataprep that helps

00:35:09.550 --> 00:35:11.560
you pre-visualize the data.

00:35:11.560 --> 00:35:16.000
And we find that this
product is really promising.

00:35:16.000 --> 00:35:20.350
On the other edge
of the pipeline,

00:35:20.350 --> 00:35:24.580
Google Data Studio
is a great solution

00:35:24.580 --> 00:35:28.390
to monitor our machine
running, basically plot

00:35:28.390 --> 00:35:32.880
the evolution of our precision
and recall of our models.

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:35.610
And there is one work that we
have started with the Google

00:35:35.610 --> 00:35:40.300
SRE team to specify some service
level indicators and service

00:35:40.300 --> 00:35:43.560
level objective to
ensure the reliability

00:35:43.560 --> 00:35:45.340
of our whole system.

00:35:45.340 --> 00:35:46.530
Also great.

00:35:46.530 --> 00:35:48.380
Thanks a lot for your attention.

00:35:48.380 --> 00:35:49.880
[APPLAUSE]

00:35:49.880 --> 00:35:52.930
